title: "Kokoro: Mood-conditioned cold-start retrieval for anime"
message: "If you use this software or its ideas, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jadhav
    given-names: Gaurav
    email: gauravmakarandjadhav@gmail.com
    alias: JGaurav26
repository-code: "https://github.com/GgauravJ05/kokoro"
url: "https://github.com/GgauravJ05/kokoro"
abstract: >-
  Kokoro learns an affective representation of anime from user review prose
  rather than from catalog metadata, using a two-tower contrastive retriever
  trained with InfoNCE. Because the item tower encodes metadata only, the model
  can rank titles with no interaction history: on a cold-start split every
  collaborative baseline scores exactly 0.0000 NDCG@10, while the trained
  content retriever reaches 0.0845 (4.97x random, +41% over an off-the-shelf
  encoder) at 4.4 ms p95. The work also reports two negative results. An
  interpretable bottleneck of named mood axes demonstrably shapes the space but
  only three of eight axes reach weak agreement with held-out tag probes. And
  the project's motivating hypothesis - that a title is better modelled as a
  mood trajectory over narrative position than as a point - is falsified on this
  corpus: a permutation control confirms a real but small position signal, while
  curve shape carries no information beyond its own mean.
keywords:
  - recommender systems
  - information retrieval
  - contrastive learning
  - affective computing
  - dense retrieval
  - interpretability
  - anime
license: AGPL-3.0-or-later
version: 0.1.0
